WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:21.290
WU: Well, hello, everyone.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.490
Thank you for coming.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.460
I know it's been
hard to get in today.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.590
We want to welcome
you to the spring

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.260
lecturer of the Undergraduate
Economics Association.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.940
I'm Charles Wu, the president.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.369
PATTY: I'm Manisha Patty.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.210
I'm the vice president.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.257
Sorry for running in.

00:00:36.257 --> 00:00:38.590
Let me now present the head
of the economics department,

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.290
Ricardo Caballero, to introduce
our distinguished lecturer

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:42.080
today.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:48.900
CABALLERO: Thank you.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.740
And welcome, you all, to this
very, very special event put

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.690
together by Teresa, Sarah,
and the Economics Undergrad's

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.520
Association.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.765
It's certainly a great honor to
introduce to you Paul Krugman.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.560
Thank you very much,
Paul, for being here.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.790
We know that your time is
quite scare these days.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.590
I think the reason,
though, he came

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.370
is because Paul is
truly an MIT guy.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.640
He did his PhD here.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.880
He has been a member of
the faculty more than once.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.390
And who knows what
the future brings, no?

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.500
But he clearly is
part of our community.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.810
And so I thank you very much.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.980
You make us very proud, Paul.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.340
Many of you may know
Paul for his very

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.620
insightful and entertaining
opinion columns

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.590
in New York Times and
many other venues.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.470
Some of us, however,
have the privilege

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:54.270
of knowing the other part
of Paul, his academic side.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:00.360
And I grew up learning
from Paul's papers.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.360
And we never had
any doubt that he

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.010
would receive the Nobel Prize.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.420
The question was when.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.180
And we're delighted that it
already happened in 2008.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.030
And again, this was the
last of a long sequence

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.000
of all the possible awards
you can get in economics.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.490
And it's pretty easy for the
insiders, for the economists,

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.320
to understand why
he got these prizes.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.150
Not only does he have
seminal contributions

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.070
in international trade
and international finance,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.730
but also reading an
academic paper by Paul,

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.160
as it is to read in a column
actually, it's a delight.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.870
Paul has probably the
largest ratio of insight

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.800
to mathematics that you can
find in academic papers.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.630
And I think the main
reason that's the case

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.330
is that he has superb
economic intuition.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.200
Truly outstanding.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.050
I would compare it to that
of our late and great Paul

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:50.580
Samuelson.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.280
These are people that can really
use math to communicate ideas.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.860
But they have seen what
they want to say well

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.120
before they saw the equations.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.273
That's pretty unique.

00:03:01.273 --> 00:03:02.940
In fact, I'm not sure
I should say this,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.600
but when I was a student
here in our graduate program,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.960
we used to joke with
some of my classmates

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.430
that many of Paul's papers had
mathematical errors in them.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.903
But they always came in pairs.

00:03:15.903 --> 00:03:17.820
And they offset each
other, so the conclusions

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.190
were always right.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.960
And that's how good
his intuition is.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.960
In any event, it's a great
honor to have you with us, Paul.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:27.769
Welcome.

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:31.122
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:36.391 --> 00:03:38.800
KRUGMAN: So Ricardo
kind of trumped

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.560
my response, which was,
the real reason that I have

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.805
such a high ratio of other
stuff to math in my papers

00:03:44.805 --> 00:03:46.680
is, I'm actually a quite
lousy mathematician.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.990
But OK.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:54.220
So I'm going to try
and talk not too long,

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.980
because my general
impression on these things

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.860
is that the back and
forth is a lot more

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.050
interesting than the lecture.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.740
As the title of
the talk implies,

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:10.510
I really want to talk some
about the state we're in,

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.420
but also some about the
intellectual state we're in.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.410
Where are we now?

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.920
And how could this happen?

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.260
Have we actually learned
anything from it?

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.950
And so on.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:24.150
So when I try and think
about the mess we're in,

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.610
I often find myself going
back to an incident that

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.860
happened back in 2002.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.770
I wasn't there, but
I've read about it.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.850
There was a conference
at the Federal Reserve

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.280
to honor Milton Friedman
on his 90th birthday.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.560
And at that conference,
Ben Bernanke,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.650
who had recently
moved to the Fed

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:56.200
from teaching at some school
or other in New Jersey,

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:01.360
addressed Milton Friedman
and Schwartz in reference

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.350
to Friedman's famous work on the
errors of the Federal Reserve,

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.840
which, according to Friedman,
was the reason that the Great

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.673
Depression happened.

00:05:08.673 --> 00:05:11.530
If the Fed had only done its
job, it wouldn't have happened.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.430
Ben Bernanke said, on behalf
of the Federal Reserve,

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.280
I'd like to say to
you, yes, you're right.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.030
We did it.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.380
We're very sorry.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.740
But thanks to you, it
won't happen again.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.160
Six years later,
it happened again.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.650
And so the question I
want to ask is, how is it

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.230
that we got into
something that bears

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:36.680
such a distinct resemblance
to the Great Depression?

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.420
What were the mistakes,
and what have we learned?

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.770
Are we actually continuing to
make some of the old mistakes?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.923
What's going on here?

00:05:45.923 --> 00:05:47.590
I guess, at some
level, the question is,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:48.798
what are economists good for?

00:05:48.798 --> 00:05:53.360
But in any case, the question
is, how did all this happen?

00:05:53.360 --> 00:06:00.100
So let me talk first about
what it is we thought we knew.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:04.810
What we thought we knew was
that the Great Depression

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:09.940
was possible first and
foremost because we

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:16.780
had an unprotected banking
system combined with a Federal

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.200
Reserve, a monetary authority,
that didn't understand

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.830
what needed to be done.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.940
And so there was a recession.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.270
There was a stock
market boom and bust.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:30.670
And there would have been
a recession regardless.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:32.920
But what made the Great
Depression so great

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.460
was banking collapse.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.628
There was a loss of confidence.

00:06:36.628 --> 00:06:38.170
People pulled money
out of the banks.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:43.810
It led to a collapse of the
financial system, panic,

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.150
disruption of markets.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.220
The Federal Reserve
failed to act forcefully.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.090
It failed to supply liquidity.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:55.000
It failed to rescue banks
that were in trouble.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.100
It failed to prevent a sharp
fall in the money supply.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.720
Although there's
a real question,

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.110
I think, given what's happened
right now, whether they really

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:05.610
could have.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.402
But certainly, the
story as Milton Friedman

00:07:07.402 --> 00:07:09.777
told it was that, if only the
Fed had done what it should

00:07:09.777 --> 00:07:12.194
have, there wouldn't have been
a shortfall of money supply

00:07:12.194 --> 00:07:14.340
and there wouldn't have
been a Great Depression.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.470
And then, going on from
there, the argument

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:21.190
is that governments
didn't understand

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.240
what needed to be done.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.680
They didn't act on it.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.740
They did some mild what we would
now call Keynesian programs.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.770
But they were never
remotely big enough,

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.850
as, actually, a former
department at MIT, E. Cary

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.760
Brown, famously showed,
that once you adjust

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.040
for cyclical effects
on the budget,

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.260
there really never was
very much fiscal expansion

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:45.720
during the '30s.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:50.200
That fiscal policy didn't work
because it was never tried.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.240
And to the extent that
there was fiscal policy,

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.220
it was halfhearted.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.460
It was pulled back.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.340
There was the famous
mistake of 1937,

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.590
when FDR was convinced
that the recovery was solid

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:05.600
and no longer needed all that
support from the government.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.510
The Federal Reserve,
similarly convinced,

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.120
tried to mop up some of those
excess reserves in the banking

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:10.810
system.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:14.500
And the result was a severe
recession, the Second Great

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.020
Depression, as many people
called it at the time--

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:20.800
all of these mistakes
that came from the past.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.610
And since we knew
about all that,

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.810
it wasn't going to happen again.

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:31.400
We had banks which were
protected by deposit insurance,

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.610
so as to rule out bank runs.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:40.780
We had regulation of banks to
go along with the insurance.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.800
We had monetary authorities
who understood the risks

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.590
and would not make the
same mistakes that banks

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:51.580
made in the '30s.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:55.480
Altogether, we had
learned the lessons.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:58.180
We were in a position not
to make those same mistakes

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.730
all over again.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.890
At the very least,
we would understand

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:05.360
that you don't allow a
plunge in the money supply.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.095
So this wasn't supposed
to be possible again.

00:09:10.095 --> 00:09:11.470
And then came our
current crisis.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:19.090
What do we understand
now about the crisis?

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.930
What we understand, I
think, is that we ended up

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:26.520
having something that was
a teched up 21st century

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.200
version of what happened in
1930, that it was very much

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.630
a replay of the same
kinds of events.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.750
Now, things look different.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:40.650
Because bank
deposits are insured,

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.730
we did not have bank runs in
the form of mobs of people

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.700
out in the street pounding
on the doors of closed bank

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.360
buildings.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:55.620
But we had the economic
equivalent of bank runs,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.910
with people rushing to
pull short-term funds out

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.790
of institutions that really
were not in a position to repay

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:06.150
those funds on short notice.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.520
So we had a financial collapse.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.930
By some measures, the
financial disruption

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.180
that took place in
2008 was as bad as what

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.880
happened in 1930, 1931.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:23.160
We had a monstrous bubble in
housing and in real estate more

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:30.150
generally coming into this,
which looked every bit as

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.637
crazy, in retrospect, as the
stock market boom of the '20s.

00:10:33.637 --> 00:10:35.220
What's really weird
is that we managed

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.470
to have that giant bubble
just a couple of years

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.870
after our own
giant stock bubble.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.585
But nonetheless, we had that,
which depressed the economy

00:10:42.585 --> 00:10:46.115
and created what would surely
have been a serious recession,

00:10:46.115 --> 00:10:48.240
even if the financial system
hadn't gone completely

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.740
bust-- but on top of
that, the major collapse

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:56.170
of the financial system.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.330
Well, all in all, it looked
a whole lot like the slump

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.390
in at the beginning of the '30s.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:08.440
You can read John Maynard
Keynes's great essay.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.020
This is six years
before he published

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.460
"The General Theory of
Employment, Interest,

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:12.060
and Money."

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:14.663
He wrote an essay called
"The Great Slump of 1930."

00:11:14.663 --> 00:11:16.080
And you really
only have to change

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.510
a few words to make
it sound like it's

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.360
a description of recent events.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.840
It was something that was
very, very similar in its form,

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:29.160
not like the great
recessions of '74, '75,

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.320
or of the early 1980s,
which were driven,

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.500
at least to a large extent, by
external events by disruption

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:35.490
of oil supply.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.670
This was a purely
internal malfunction.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:43.650
It was, as Keynes wrote in
1930, magneto trouble, which

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:44.733
turns out to be actually--

00:11:44.733 --> 00:11:46.275
I thought it was
alternator, but it's

00:11:46.275 --> 00:11:48.880
more like the early version
of a spark plug or something.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.640
But anyway, a small
part in the car

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.162
malfunctioned but it brought
the whole engine to a stop.

00:11:53.162 --> 00:11:54.370
And we did it all over again.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.710
How is that possible?

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.680
A part of the
answer is that we--

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.300
actually, let me say it
a little differently.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:16.030
What I say is that we had a
combination of mislearning

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:18.430
the lessons of the
Great Depression,

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:24.700
forgetting the lessons,
and failing to generalize--

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.070
excessive literal-mindedness.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:31.480
So let me go through
those in some sequence.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:38.630
In terms of mislearning,
we developed a conviction

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:43.400
that the Federal Reserve
or its counterparts

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:48.650
abroad can always do whatever
is necessary to stop a slump.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:52.190
The interpretation, not at the
time, but some years later,

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:54.770
by the time that Milton
Friedman was in his heyday,

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.870
was that people had
become convinced

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:59.533
that, if only the Federal
Reserve had been more

00:12:59.533 --> 00:13:00.950
aggressive, that
would easily have

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.242
prevented the Great Depression.

00:13:02.242 --> 00:13:05.210
And since we had smarter, better
central bankers these days,

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:07.310
we were never going to
experience that again.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:15.080
Even before this
hit, if you were

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.940
looking at events outside
the United States,

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:21.710
you realized that that was
probably a misinterpretation,

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:26.780
that, yes, the Fed can
always print lots of money.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:30.410
But if short-term
interest rates go to zero,

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:32.300
further money printing
doesn't do anything.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:34.580
It has no traction.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:35.570
It's not clear.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:39.860
When Friedman and
Schwartz showed

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:42.930
that there was a big
decline the money supply,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:44.900
they used a broad
measure of money,

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:49.730
which was a variety of
deposits, as well as just

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:52.260
currency and bank reserves.

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.310
And it was just presumed that
the Fed could have stopped that

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.212
from falling.

00:13:56.212 --> 00:13:57.920
It's not at all clear
that it could have.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.600
And we could see
that in a way now,

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.080
where there's vast amounts
of bank reserves out there

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.520
which are not being lent out.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.520
And so we probably
mislearned some

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.360
of the lessons of the 1930s.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.060
There was an unwarranted
belief that it

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:13.850
was easy for
technical operations

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:18.290
by the Federal Reserve
to prevent this crisis.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.120
We forgot some of the lessons.

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.910
I think people simply
forgot just how

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.640
unstable financial
markets can be.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.960
They forgot how difficult it can
be to get policy traction when

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.300
things are really bad.

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:39.080
It's another example
of reading old stuff.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:44.960
You can acquire a newly printed
edition of the 1948 edition

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:48.800
of Paul Samuelson's
economics textbook.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:50.300
And it's really
interesting reading.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.050
And particularly, if
you read the sections

00:14:52.050 --> 00:15:00.230
about the limits of monetary
policy in fighting slumps,

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:01.700
it sounds exactly right.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:04.530
It sounds exactly like what
we're going through right now.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:08.120
So Samuelson in 1948 had
a really good appreciation

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:09.350
of the limits of policy.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:10.910
But we forgot that.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:15.330
By the time I started writing
a principles of economics

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:18.050
textbook, all of those
caveats, all of those cautions

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.750
were gone, and the
general view was

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:22.310
of the omnipotence
of central banks,

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:24.950
at least in their
ability to create demand.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.500
And so we forgot lessons.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.010
We forgot stuff
that we had known.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.900
I'll be talking more
about that forgetting

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.770
in a few more minutes.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:45.430
And literalness-- we
did know that banks

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.430
are subject to bank
runs, although we

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:49.780
forgot just how serious.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:51.550
There was a big
move to deregulate

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:57.640
banks, which was based on
forgetting just how badly

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.160
they'd gone awry in the past.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.862
But mainly, what happened was--

00:16:02.862 --> 00:16:05.320
and I'm guilty of this too--
you looked out there and said,

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.810
well, but we have
deposit insurance.

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:08.770
We have guarantees.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:15.310
We have credit lines
from the Federal Reserve.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.890
Banks can go bad,
but we can't have

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:22.360
the kinds of destructive bank
runs, the kind of disruptive

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:27.340
failure of the banking system
that we had in the 1930s,

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:30.490
because we do have institutions
to protect against that.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:33.070
The trouble was, of course--

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.070
now it's "of
course--" that we were

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:40.240
excessively literal in our idea
of what it is to be a bank.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:44.240
We thought of a bank as
being, well, basically,

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:48.700
a big marble building with a
row of tellers and Jimmy Stewart

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:53.110
running it, whereas,
in fact, of course,

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.300
we should have known better.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:17:01.480
Even in your basic economic
models of bank runs,

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:03.340
the bank that appears
in those models

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:05.900
is not defined by
those characteristics.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:09.280
The bank is defined by borrowing
short and lending long,

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.829
by creating liquid assets
that people can regard

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:15.760
as money, while, at the
same time, using the money

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:17.980
placed in their care
to make investments

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.470
that can't be turned into
cash on short notice.

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:24.280
And by that standard,
lots of things are banks.

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.710
These days now, everybody
talks about the shadow banking

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.650
system, all of the institutions
that were effectively banks

00:17:29.650 --> 00:17:30.760
but were not--

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:33.310
depository institutions.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.823
Until the crisis hit, no one
was thinking much about that.

00:17:35.823 --> 00:17:37.240
And so we were
caught by surprise.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:41.680
It turned out that you
could have bank runs just

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:45.640
like the old ones except
not quite so visible

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.260
because we had all of these
things that were banks,

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:51.258
but not banks in the
old sense, and subject

00:17:51.258 --> 00:17:53.050
to the same kind of
collapse of confidence.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:55.570
So instead of mobs of
people in the street,

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:57.790
you had mobs of
investors refusing

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:00.460
to roll over their repos.

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:01.780
But the effects were the same.

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:05.290
And so we were
caught by surprise

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.410
by this terrifying crisis.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:19.084
The actual slump has not been
as bad as the Great Depression.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:26.500
And there's been some
self-congratulation about that,

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:27.940
probably excessive.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.480
I'm not sure.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:35.530
I have extremely high regard for
my former department chairman.

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:39.018
But I'm not sure that "the
world didn't end on his watch"

00:18:39.018 --> 00:18:41.560
is a good enough reason to make
Ben Bernanke man of the year.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:48.370
But things have been
not as bad as they

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.490
were in the Great Depression.

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:54.040
And that is an achievement.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:02.650
If we look at the first
year, globally, of the slump,

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.960
it actually was comparable
to the Great Depression.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:07.315
Industrial production
in the first 12 months

00:19:07.315 --> 00:19:09.190
will tell us as much
this time around

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:12.670
as it did from '29 to '30.

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:14.860
World Trade fell
faster than it did

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:17.440
in the first year of
the Great Depression,

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:18.670
but then it did level off.

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:23.680
So what it looks like is
that the shock to the system

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:26.080
was every bit as bad as it
was in the Great Depression,

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.930
but the system handled it
better this time around.

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:31.040
Why was that?

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:36.130
Well, part of the answer was,
we have learned some things,

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:42.640
although there were
some obvious failures

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:45.850
to understand the
depth of the problem.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:50.770
The failure to rescue Lehman was
obviously a huge misjudgment,

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:53.980
sort of the 21st century
equivalent of the failure

00:19:53.980 --> 00:20:03.430
to rescue the Bank of the
United States in 1930.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.500
Nonetheless, there
was willingness

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:08.230
to step in and rescue key
financial institutions.

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:11.890
People stepped in to protect the
system from a total meltdown.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.140
And that certainly helped.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:19.430
I suspect that a very large part
of the reason we didn't have

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:23.030
a full replay of the
Great Depression, though,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:24.140
is actually not that.

00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:28.250
Although I wouldn't want to try
the experiment of letting more

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.470
financial institutions
go back and do it again

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:34.910
and let even more stuff fail and
not bail out AIG and all that.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:39.350
Probably a very large part of
what went right, or at least

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.810
didn't go as wrong as
it might have this time,

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.720
was simply the fact that we
have much bigger governments

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:46.980
than we did 80 years ago.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:52.010
That's an underappreciated
aspect of the story.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:56.990
Big government in the crisis has
been a help almost independent

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:00.770
of what the governments do,
because at least they're there,

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:03.810
so that when
production is falling,

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:08.000
and private sector
employment is falling,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.660
and people are nervous,
and nobody wants to spend,

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.160
the social security
checks are still

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:15.990
going out Medicare is still
paying the health care bills.

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:18.680
There's just a large
automatic stabilizer role

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:22.760
of government, which has almost
surely been a major factor

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.200
in cushioning the crisis.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.950
If we hadn't had that,
it could quite easily

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:33.530
have been a much,
much deeper slump.

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:37.160
And then there have been some
deliberate government efforts

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:40.010
to prop up demand.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:41.960
Yes, we have a stimulus.

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.060
Yes, we would probably
have 12% unemployment

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:46.415
now if we hadn't had the
stimulus in the United States.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:51.230
But the efforts
to prop up demand

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:55.250
have been, on the whole,
probably a smaller factor

00:21:55.250 --> 00:22:07.430
in what's gone right
than the simple existence

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:13.365
of big government and
the moves to stabilize

00:22:13.365 --> 00:22:14.240
the financial sector.

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:22.860
Still, I don't think you should
want to view this as a success

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:24.700
story, even so far.

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.200
What we did was,
despite everything we

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.180
were supposed to
have learned, we

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:35.550
managed to do something that was
at least a pretty significant

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:38.670
echo of the Great Depression.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:43.290
Maybe it wasn't the
same total collapse.

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:44.910
Unemployment wasn't
quite so bad.

00:22:50.412 --> 00:22:52.620
I was about to say there
aren't people selling apples

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:53.203
on the street.

00:22:53.203 --> 00:22:55.110
But actually, there
are a lot of people

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:56.460
selling whatever on the street.

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.870
I don't, obviously,
personally, have any memories

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.190
of New York in the '30s.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:10.110
But in New York in early
2010 looks pretty bad.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:15.190
It's been a quite serious
devastation to the economy.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:18.330
It's not supposed
to have happened.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:25.230
Furthermore, this
thing ain't over.

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:29.070
One of the difficult
things, I think,

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:32.190
that people have in
wrapping their minds around

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:36.670
is that the end of a recession
is not the end of the slump,

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:39.400
that, while it's
almost certainly true

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:41.800
that the National Bureau
of Economic Research

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:45.250
will eventually declare
that the recession ended

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:49.840
in June or July of
2009, unemployment

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:51.350
has continued to rise--

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:53.980
yes, it was down
slightly this morning,

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:57.400
but that's statistical
noise, almost surely--

00:23:57.400 --> 00:24:04.810
and that most of us
do not think that it's

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:11.590
going to come down
quickly at all, that we're

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:14.050
likely to be facing
probably years

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:18.520
of high unemployment, which
is years of huge suffering

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:22.810
in the population, years
that will leave really

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:27.700
long-term scars on our society.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.890
The line I was about to
use-- well, I'll use it--

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:33.910
is the worst hit, probably,
in terms of lifetime impact,

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:37.420
are young people
graduating from college

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:38.930
facing a terrible job market.

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:44.530
And what the statistical
estimates show is that,

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:50.290
basically, if you graduate
into a bad job market,

00:24:50.290 --> 00:24:53.830
you will still be worse off than
if you graduate into a good job

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:55.823
market even 20 years later.

00:24:55.823 --> 00:24:57.490
So there will be
really long-term damage

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:58.198
from all of this.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.200
Sorry about that, guys.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:07.050
So everyone leave the room
and go polish up your resumes.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.888
Something else is
happening again too.

00:25:18.888 --> 00:25:21.180
I think we're now past the
point of saying, oh, my god,

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:25.410
how did we manage to
reproduce the situation that

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:29.430
led to the great crash of 1930?

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:37.260
But ever since the GDP
seemed to stabilize,

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:39.350
the economy seemed
to end its freefall.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:41.280
Now, everyone has
been saying, well,

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:43.225
the thing now is to
make sure we don't

00:25:43.225 --> 00:25:45.600
repeat the mistakes that were
made later during the Great

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:47.400
Depression.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:52.380
Don't repeat the mistake
of pulling out the support

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:54.240
from the economy when
the recovery really

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:59.880
wasn't solidly in place
and sending the economy

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:01.200
into a second slump.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.440
So we're not going
to do 1937 again.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:08.910
Everyone says that, lots
of people on the outside.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:12.180
Christy Romer, the head of the
Council of Economic Advisors,

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:16.080
has even published an article,
"The Lessons of 1937,"

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:20.110
explaining why we really
don't want to do that.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:21.810
The fact is, at
least at the moment,

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:25.110
it appears that we
are going to do that.

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:28.080
It's almost baked
in at this point.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:34.260
The stimulus reaches
its peak impact

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:38.100
on the economy, as best
we can tell, this summer,

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.890
and then starts to fade out.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:43.320
All indications are
that unemployment

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.210
will remain very high.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:47.520
The economy is going to
be still deep in the hole.

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:55.660
The Federal Reserve is going to
come to an end of its mortgage

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:58.240
purchase program, which
is one of the main things

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:01.333
that it's been doing beyond
just keeping interest rates low.

00:27:01.333 --> 00:27:03.250
Again, that's going to
happen in a few months.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:06.350
The economy is still
deep in the hole.

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:14.050
If you look at the budget that
the administration offered last

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:15.760
wee-- this week--

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:17.270
I'm losing my track of time.

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:19.720
Anyway, when you
look at the budget,

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:25.850
it actually predicts or calls
for substantial cutbacks

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:29.930
in government spending, mainly
as a result of the expiration

00:27:29.930 --> 00:27:32.600
of the fading out
of the stimulus,

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:34.620
but also because
there's now to be

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:40.580
a freeze on nonsecurity
discretionary spending, which

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:43.670
is to appease the deficit hawks.

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:47.880
All of that adds up to a
pretty significant turn

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:50.580
towards contractionary policy
taking place over the next year

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:54.750
or so in the face of a
deeply depressed economy.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:59.620
Will that actually send us
into a double dip recession?

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:02.233
I think the technical
answer is, God knows.

00:28:02.233 --> 00:28:03.900
There's so much going
on in the economy,

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:06.490
it's never possible
to really know.

00:28:06.490 --> 00:28:09.960
But we do know,
anyway, that policy

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:14.400
is acting as if the problem
has been just about solved,

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.980
whereas the reality is,
mass unemployment is

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:21.780
a likely feature of the economy
for quite some time to come.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:28.475
What I think we really
failed to learn,

00:28:28.475 --> 00:28:29.850
or certainly, at
least in policy,

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:33.240
we're not responding to,
is that, historically,

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.400
the aftermath of
financial crises

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:38.410
is a very, very prolonged
period of high unemployment,

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:40.110
that it's not something
you solve just

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:43.698
by stepping in and preventing
the freefall of the economy.

00:28:43.698 --> 00:28:45.990
It's something that is going
to require a lot of action

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:49.592
to support the economy
for an extended period.

00:28:49.592 --> 00:28:51.300
Anybody who is interested
in these things

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:54.570
should look at the work of
Carmen Reinhart and Ken Rogoff,

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:59.310
who have studied past financial
crises and produced summary

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:00.240
statistics.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:06.600
What Rogoff says is that we are
actually experiencing a garden

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:10.710
variety severe financial
crisis, which sounds oxymoronic.

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:13.320
But what he means by that
is that the United States

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:16.720
is more or less so far tracking
right down the average.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.300
Take a severe financial crisis.

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.430
The United States is looking
just like the average.

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:21.930
And in the average
financial crisis,

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:25.800
unemployment rises
for almost five years

00:29:25.800 --> 00:29:27.580
before it starts turning down.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:29.700
So we are looking at a
really prolonged period

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:34.320
of high unemployment if
history is any guide.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.770
And yet we are actually starting
to withdraw policy support

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:41.418
from the economy quite soon.

00:29:41.418 --> 00:29:42.210
So what's going on?

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:52.180
How can we be replaying
the past so badly.

00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:57.990
That's really the question
that has worried me a lot.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:04.590
Part of the answer is politics.

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:11.010
Obviously, I think we've all
learned a pretty harsh lesson

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:13.470
in all of this.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.990
We've learned that, if
you're an economist,

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:18.810
you tend to say,
well, let's figure out

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:22.650
what the optimal
policy is, and the job

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:25.770
is done once you've
explained what that is.

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:28.140
In reality, of
course, things have

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:30.930
to go through a
legislative process.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:33.468
It can be very
difficult to do things,

00:30:33.468 --> 00:30:35.010
even if you're quite
sure they should

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.770
be done, because other
people don't agree,

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:40.380
or other people actually
even might privately

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:43.440
believe they should be done, but
not with the other guy sitting

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:44.610
in the White House.

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:50.230
So there's a real difficulty
in getting things through.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:58.520
There's a lot of
difficulty also in getting

00:30:58.520 --> 00:31:03.290
what you do understand
economically past even

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:04.700
sympathetic politicians.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:12.700
A year ago, there
was always a lot

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:16.840
of discussion, discussion
among the people

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:18.970
who were actually going
to make the policy bill--

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:23.860
so discussion kibitzing from
the outside and some meetings

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:27.440
and phone conversations
and so on.

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:32.440
The political people,
who know their job, who

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:35.230
think they know what they can
get through Congress and so on,

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:38.650
tend to always look for partway.

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:40.670
They tend to look
for, if you say

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:43.510
we need to do this big
thing, well, that's hard.

00:31:43.510 --> 00:31:44.650
Let's do part of it.

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:50.860
Their instinct is
always to do, well,

00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:54.370
part of what the economists
think ought to be done.

00:31:54.370 --> 00:31:56.620
It's very difficult to
deal with a situation

00:31:56.620 --> 00:32:01.435
where actually half a loaf may
be not much better than none.

00:32:01.435 --> 00:32:03.310
And that is kind of the
situation when you're

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:04.610
faced with this kind of crisis.

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:08.500
Because if you do a halfhearted
policy, although, economically,

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:11.710
it may help, if the
economy still looks lousy

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:13.682
after you've done the
halfhearted policy,

00:32:13.682 --> 00:32:15.640
the conclusion of the
political process is not,

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:17.140
well, we need to do more of it.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:19.250
The conclusion is, well,
that policy failed,

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:20.450
so we can't do anymore.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:23.350
And so that's been very much
the case in the stimulus.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:25.780
I can give you chapter and
verse on why the economy would

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:27.940
be in much worse shape
if the stimulus hadn't

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.180
been carried out.

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:32.320
But the fact that unemployment
is still close to 10%

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:33.850
means that, in
Washington, the view

00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:37.390
is, well, we've just proved
that stimulus doesn't work.

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:39.075
And that was
somewhat foreseeable,

00:32:39.075 --> 00:32:40.450
but it was impossible
to get that

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:46.360
across to the political people,
that doing what you think

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:48.790
of as a more careful,
cautious policy

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:51.070
is actually extremely risky,
because you really have

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:54.460
one shot at this and no more.

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:58.950
Last but not least,
the economists

00:32:58.950 --> 00:32:59.950
themselves are confused.

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:05.080
A lot of people
probably read the piece

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:09.415
I wrote for the Times Magazine.

00:33:09.415 --> 00:33:14.530
It had been really amazing
within the economics profession

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:21.100
to see how much has been lost,
not by everybody, of course.

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:23.980
But I've used the
phrase that we're

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:26.495
living in a Dark Age
of macroeconomics.

00:33:26.495 --> 00:33:28.120
And what I mean by
that is that there's

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:31.450
a difference between the
Dark Age and barbarism.

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:33.310
Barbarism was, they
never knew better.

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:36.040
Dark Age is, the people forgot
what the Greeks and the Romans

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:36.970
knew.

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:39.850
And to some extent, that's
where we are in economics now.

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:47.050
It's been really astonishing
to watch economists reinventing

00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:52.157
1930 vintage
fallacies as if they

00:33:52.157 --> 00:33:54.490
were fresh insights, because
they don't know about this.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:03.040
There are real
disputes you can argue.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:04.660
There are respectable
arguments that we should not

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:06.317
have done a fiscal
stimulus, or that we

00:34:06.317 --> 00:34:08.900
should have done something very
different from what Obama did.

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:11.139
But many of the arguments
against stimulus

00:34:11.139 --> 00:34:16.060
are simple failure to understand
very basic insights that

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:18.840
were hard-won, but
hard-won 70 years ago

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:22.690
and, unfortunately, have
been lost in the interim.

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:27.000
So we came into this
deeply unprepared.

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:28.030
What happens now?

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:31.330
I don't know.

00:34:31.330 --> 00:34:36.790
I'm actually really worried
about where we are now.

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:43.810
If you want to look at the
situation, the first year

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:48.520
of crisis, we're at just
like what Keynes described

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:51.880
in "The Great Slump of 1930."

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:55.060
So we managed to replicate
the crisis amazingly.

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:00.510
When Keynes wrote his, again,
masterwork, "The General Theory

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:02.070
of Employment,
Interest, and Money,"

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:04.022
it was not during the
acute phase of crisis.

00:35:04.022 --> 00:35:05.730
It was not when the
economy was slumping.

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:07.105
It was actually
written at a time

00:35:07.105 --> 00:35:09.810
when there had been a
significant but incomplete

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:11.520
recovery.

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:14.100
And what he was
worried about then

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:17.670
was not so much, why does the
economy fall off the edge?

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:20.730
In fact, one of his
great strategic decisions

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:23.910
was, I'm not going to explain
why depressions happen.

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:26.610
I'm going to try to
explain why they continue,

00:35:26.610 --> 00:35:29.310
why the economy can
remain stuck in a period

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:32.700
of high unemployment
for many years on end.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.800
That's the world
we're in right now.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:36.900
We're in a world
where, at the moment,

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:40.328
it's hard to see any drivers
for a really vigorous recovery.

00:35:40.328 --> 00:35:42.120
If you ask yourself
what's going to happen,

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:47.655
something might come along, but
consumers are heavily indebted.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.907
Firms are in much better shape.

00:35:52.907 --> 00:35:54.490
Corporations are in
much better shape.

00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:56.073
But they have lots
of excess capacity.

00:35:56.073 --> 00:35:57.595
Why would they invest?

00:35:57.595 --> 00:35:59.220
Where is the demand
going to come from?

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:00.762
Well, it could come
from governments.

00:36:00.762 --> 00:36:03.330
But politically, we're
actually kind of stalled.

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:04.380
There is no sentiment.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:07.650
Instead, there's a great
deal of worry about deficits

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:10.020
and a lot of pressure to cut
spending, not increase it.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:12.660
So we are somewhat caught
in a situation that

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:15.630
is more than a little
bit reminiscent not

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:18.900
of the onset of the Great
Depression, but the mid-1930s,

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:21.930
where you were really
stuck in a holding

00:36:21.930 --> 00:36:26.850
pattern with mass unemployment
just becoming a fact of life.

00:36:31.730 --> 00:36:35.420
I'd like to say that
everybody is really

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:40.060
rethinking their positions
in this environment.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:41.510
There's not much sign of that.

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:45.140
What actually seems
to be happening,

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:47.240
as far as I can make
out, is that, by avoiding

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.940
utter disaster, we've
actually also managed

00:36:49.940 --> 00:36:52.190
to avoid being
forced to confront

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:53.795
our own intellectual failings.

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:57.930
I would not believe
this a year ago,

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:00.513
but when I look at the state of
discussion, both in Washington

00:37:00.513 --> 00:37:03.200
and within the economics
profession, what

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:09.400
is amazing to me is, much of
it is as if it were still 2007.

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:10.780
And we've gone back to it.

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:12.670
People are espousing
the same positions.

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:15.910
People are saying the same
things, the same rhetoric

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:20.620
about private sector,
dynamism, and the evils

00:37:20.620 --> 00:37:22.420
of big government.

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:29.350
The same denunciations
of Keynesian economics

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:30.670
are right back in vogue.

00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:33.630
Nothing much has changed.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:36.610
And there doesn't seem to be
an obvious forcing event that

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:43.345
will change either the economy
or the intellectual climate.

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:49.300
Something will
eventually come along.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:57.100
In the long run, we
will work this out,

00:37:57.100 --> 00:37:59.990
and we'll find a
way to recovery.

00:37:59.990 --> 00:38:03.440
But you know how
this is going to end.

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:05.700
John Maynard Keynes is my
great idol in economics.

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:08.863
And what he said about the
long run is, in the long run,

00:38:08.863 --> 00:38:09.530
we are all dead.

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:16.160
And I left a little thing
on my notes here saying,

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:18.327
come up with something
optimistic to say at the end.

00:38:18.327 --> 00:38:20.820
But I haven't
managed to find it.

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.436
So with that, I'm going to
throw it up for Q&amp;A. Thank you.

00:38:23.436 --> 00:38:26.908
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:37.324 --> 00:38:39.490
WU: So thank you very
much, Professor Krugman,

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:40.750
for a wonderful talk.

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:42.167
Another round of
applause for him.

00:38:42.167 --> 00:38:44.991
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:47.306 --> 00:38:50.000
PATTY: We have a small
gift for Professor Krugman.

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:54.073
It is our t-shirt
that we sell for UEA.

00:38:54.073 --> 00:38:55.490
And we would like
you to have it--

00:38:55.490 --> 00:38:55.760
KRUGMAN: Thank you.

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:57.090
PATTY: --to remember us by.

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:58.750
KRUGMAN: Thank you.

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:01.205
WU: And Professor Krugman
has agreed to a few questions

00:39:01.205 --> 00:39:01.830
from the floor.

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:04.070
So please raise your
hands and speak clearly.

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:08.188
OK, Yeah.

00:39:08.188 --> 00:39:10.940
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.830
What are the strengths
and weaknesses

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:15.300
of the fractional
reserve system?

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:18.470
And do you see value in any
of the alternatives to it?

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:18.970
KRUGMAN: OK.

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:21.710
The question was, what do I
think of the fractional reserve

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:24.860
system, and are there
any alternatives, which

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:28.830
means we have banks that
accept deposits and lend out

00:39:28.830 --> 00:39:30.560
most of the money,
but only part of it

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:32.480
in the form of liquid reserves.

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:37.490
And there have been many
people, over the centuries, who

00:39:37.490 --> 00:39:38.990
have argued that
that's a bad thing,

00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:43.610
and that, basically, banks
should be safes, depositories

00:39:43.610 --> 00:39:44.240
for cash.

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:51.067
You cannot do away
with that system.

00:39:51.067 --> 00:39:52.400
Remember everything I just said.

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:58.080
We don't get to define
legally what is a bank.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:00.980
We get to define
legally what's going

00:40:00.980 --> 00:40:02.280
to have deposit insurance.

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:05.040
We get to define legally
who obeys what regulations.

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:08.490
But if you say that banks and
depository institutions are not

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:10.650
going to be allowed
to do anything really

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:12.870
with your money,
then what will happen

00:40:12.870 --> 00:40:16.200
is that more and
more resources will

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:19.050
flow to other
institutions that give you

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:22.000
ready access to your money,
but actually put it to work.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:24.420
So actually, it's something
like fractional reserve.

00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:26.850
Banking is going to emerge
whether you like it or not.

00:40:30.792 --> 00:40:32.130
So you can't really ban that.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:35.040
What you can do is, you can try
to make sure that at least most

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:37.860
of the people who are
doing bank type activities

00:40:37.860 --> 00:40:43.140
are caught within a-- are both
circumscribed in what they do

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:45.490
and have guarantees behind them.

00:40:45.490 --> 00:40:48.450
So the trick is not to say
that we're going to make--

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:51.120
we're going to go to
100% reserve banking,

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:52.747
because if you do
that, what we'll do

00:40:52.747 --> 00:40:54.330
is you create a
perfectly safe banking

00:40:54.330 --> 00:40:57.637
sector, which is totally
irrelevant to the economy.

00:40:57.637 --> 00:40:59.220
And meanwhile, the
real banking sector

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:02.040
will be subject to all
the crises as before.

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.080
What you can do instead
is to try and make sure

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:08.550
that the actual financial
system you have has got

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:12.000
limits on leverage, capital
requirements, guarantees,

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:17.660
a resolution procedure,
prudential requirements.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:19.380
Basically, what
we need to do is,

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:21.690
we need to recreate
a 21st century

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:25.770
version of the '30s, of the New
Deal banking reforms, which,

00:41:25.770 --> 00:41:28.920
we should remember,
produced half a century

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:30.343
without major financial crises.

00:41:30.343 --> 00:41:32.260
So it's not that we don't
know how to do this.

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:34.960
It's just that we
don't want to do it,

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:40.680
or at least that there's an
awful lot of money and ideology

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.005
standing in the way of doing it.

00:41:43.005 --> 00:41:44.400
Yeah.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:47.840
AUDIENCE: What do you
think about the idea

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:53.700
that China and the United States
could determine the economy

00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:56.168
for the 21st century?

00:41:56.168 --> 00:41:58.697
And the question is
how that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:58.697 --> 00:42:00.530
KRUGMAN: I guess the
question was, what do I

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:02.180
think about China
and the United States

00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:04.830
determining the economy
of the 21st century?

00:42:07.155 --> 00:42:09.530
There are other people out
there, I guess, is the answer.

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:16.100
Europe is essentially
coequal with the United

00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:19.560
States as a player,
for good and for real.

00:42:19.560 --> 00:42:21.560
When they do well, it's
good for the world.

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:26.480
World trade policy, which
is my original home field,

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:30.800
is set in Brussels and
Washington, not in Washington.

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:33.710
Brussels is every bit as
powerful in those issues

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:34.850
as we are.

00:42:34.850 --> 00:42:38.960
India is an increasingly
large player.

00:42:38.960 --> 00:42:42.110
Japan is not
negligible, even now.

00:42:42.110 --> 00:42:44.650
And so no, I don't
think that's the way.

00:42:44.650 --> 00:42:47.360
Now, we have a
big problem, which

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:52.790
is that China has become a
major economic power while still

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:57.710
being quite poor, and also
while not actually operating

00:42:57.710 --> 00:42:59.060
the same way as the others do.

00:42:59.060 --> 00:43:02.390
Let me put it that way.

00:43:02.390 --> 00:43:04.670
Every two years, the
Treasury Department

00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:08.150
is obliged to report on whether
China is middling its currency.

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:10.970
And every two
years, the Treasury

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:14.370
lies, because of
course they are.

00:43:14.370 --> 00:43:16.820
And no, they had
reasons why they

00:43:16.820 --> 00:43:18.035
thought it was a good idea.

00:43:18.035 --> 00:43:19.910
But it becomes a real
problem now, especially

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:20.827
in this kind of world.

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:28.070
We are in a depressed world,
where the normal policies have

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:31.670
lost traction, which means
that, if one country pursues

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:35.300
that undervalued exchange rate
and runs an artificial trade

00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:37.400
surplus, that really
is a predatory policy

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:38.760
against the rest of the world.

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:40.427
So that's the issues
that are coming up.

00:43:40.427 --> 00:43:43.910
And then, of course,
environment--

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:48.170
so China is now
number one on least

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:51.180
one thing, which is
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:56.570
So we're going to have to find
a way to work out a system.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:59.150
All of our setup, all
of our institutions,

00:43:59.150 --> 00:44:02.060
are still really
designed for a world

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:03.860
in which a handful
of rich countries

00:44:03.860 --> 00:44:05.420
dominate the world economy.

00:44:05.420 --> 00:44:06.920
And we haven't yet
figured out a way

00:44:06.920 --> 00:44:11.700
to deal with big
but poor economies.

00:44:11.700 --> 00:44:13.840
Yeah, up there.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.143
Yeah.

00:44:15.143 --> 00:44:17.060
AUDIENCE: What are your
thoughts on the future

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:20.090
of the euro with the huge
debt prices and Spain

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:22.465
and Portugal's economic woes?

00:44:22.465 --> 00:44:24.090
KRUGMAN: All right,
future of the euro.

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:30.650
It's funny.

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:39.060
When I was thinking
about the last few days,

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:44.883
I'm very fond of speaking to
eurocrats, to people who work

00:44:44.883 --> 00:44:46.050
for the European Commission.

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:48.330
Because they're actually
kind of fun once

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:50.730
you learn how to do the talk.

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:54.330
They're very smart, but
they're also straight

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:55.620
out of "Yes Minister."

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:01.200
And they talk in these
certain locutions

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:02.937
which need to be translated.

00:45:02.937 --> 00:45:04.770
So the other parallel
I use is that I always

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:08.070
think it's like the scene
in Annie Hall, where they're

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:09.570
having a conversation
in English,

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:13.440
but there are subtitles with
what they're really saying.

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:18.380
And so I remember that,
back in the early '90s,

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:20.340
I'd have conversation
with eurocrats.

00:45:20.340 --> 00:45:25.080
And they would say these
elegantly incomprehensible

00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:28.570
things about widening versus
deepening and priorities.

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:31.050
And you finally realized that
the subtitle actually read,

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:32.675
we should never have
let the Greeks in.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:43.410
So what I think about Greece
is that that's actually

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:47.040
a case of hard cases
making bad law.

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:50.910
Greece actually has been
fiscally responsible

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:52.620
from time immemorial.

00:45:52.620 --> 00:45:55.230
It's been in default
half the time

00:45:55.230 --> 00:45:57.150
every other year
since independence.

00:45:57.150 --> 00:46:01.050
So Greece was sort of
bound to be an accident.

00:46:01.050 --> 00:46:02.910
And that's not really
the revealing case,

00:46:02.910 --> 00:46:06.480
although now there's some
contagion from Greece.

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:08.070
The real story and
the reason that you

00:46:08.070 --> 00:46:10.950
need to be worried
about the euro is Spain.

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:14.070
Spain actually behaved
quite responsibly.

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:20.600
Just a couple of years ago,
they had a budget surplus.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:22.770
They had relatively
low government debt

00:46:22.770 --> 00:46:24.340
as a share of GDP.

00:46:24.340 --> 00:46:26.980
They did all the things
you're supposed to do.

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:30.540
But they had enormous
real estate boom.

00:46:33.425 --> 00:46:34.800
It's not actually
clear what they

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:36.660
could have done about that,
even if they had chosen to.

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:38.670
But anyway, all the
respectable people

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:41.340
were telling them that
there was no problem.

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:44.850
And then when the
boom went bust,

00:46:44.850 --> 00:46:49.080
they have no adjustment
mechanism available.

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:52.830
They really need to have their
manufacturing become much more

00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:55.830
competitive, compared
with Germany and France.

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:58.980
But they can't devalue
their currency,

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:01.892
because they don't have one.

00:47:01.892 --> 00:47:03.600
Alternatively, they
could get some relief

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:06.360
if there was a lot of
out-migration of labor,

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:08.730
as happens when individual
US states are depressed.

00:47:08.730 --> 00:47:11.820
But of course, although there's
legally free movement of labor

00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:15.360
within Europe, in practical
terms, it's not that big.

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:18.690
So what's happening to Spain,
which now has a massive budget

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:23.550
deficit, but only because
of the depressed economy,

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:26.400
is exactly what euro skeptics--
people who were not sure

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:28.850
that Europe was ready for
a single currency-- said

00:47:28.850 --> 00:47:29.760
would happen.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:31.110
And that is a huge problem.

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:33.070
We're having exactly
the nightmare scenario,

00:47:33.070 --> 00:47:36.660
the asymmetric shock
to a major eurozone

00:47:36.660 --> 00:47:40.200
economy with no adjustment
mechanism available.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:41.550
It's not how it's going to work.

00:47:41.550 --> 00:47:43.470
No fiscal integration.

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:46.380
When Florida has
a terrible slump,

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:48.330
the budgetary consequences,
while terrible,

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:50.070
are not as bad as
they might have been,

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:52.440
because the Social
Security and Medicare

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:54.060
checks come from Washington.

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:57.030
Spanish social insurance checks
don't come from Brussels.

00:47:57.030 --> 00:48:00.540
So everything that you worried
about that might go wrong

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:02.190
from this project
of a single currency

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:03.527
is going wrong for Spain.

00:48:03.527 --> 00:48:05.610
Now, what that means about
the future of the euro,

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:06.152
I don't know.

00:48:06.152 --> 00:48:13.530
Because it is a pretty good
example of why maybe they

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:15.030
should have had
some second thoughts

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:17.410
about the whole project.

00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:24.330
But dropping out of the
euro is almost infeasible.

00:48:24.330 --> 00:48:26.370
It's not clear how
anyone could do that.

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:33.690
The last time we've seen a major
single currency area break up,

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:35.280
as far as I know--

00:48:35.280 --> 00:48:37.170
Peter may know
better, [MUTTERING]----

00:48:37.170 --> 00:48:39.628
but I think it's the breakup
of the Austro-Hungarian Empire

00:48:39.628 --> 00:48:44.350
at the end of World War
I. And that was done by--

00:48:44.350 --> 00:48:46.110
they closed the borders
for several weeks

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:48.470
while all the
currency was exchanged

00:48:48.470 --> 00:48:51.270
and the banks' deposits
were converted.

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:56.580
And I don't think you can
do that in the modern world.

00:48:56.580 --> 00:49:00.910
So we really have a
very ugly problem.

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:06.810
My guess is that there might be
some at least limited defaults

00:49:06.810 --> 00:49:08.370
by some eurozone governments.

00:49:08.370 --> 00:49:10.440
It probably doesn't
bring down the euro.

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:11.970
The zone probably
doesn't break up,

00:49:11.970 --> 00:49:13.553
because it's very
hard see how it can.

00:49:13.553 --> 00:49:16.030
But this is ugly.

00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:18.800
This is what we were afraid of.

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:25.517
And I really feel sorry for
Spain, which really, again,

00:49:25.517 --> 00:49:27.100
played by all the
rules, and then just

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:31.780
got sideswiped by the
combination of a huge bubble

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:36.880
and the structural problems
of the single currency.

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:41.120
Yeah.

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:42.593
Yeah.

00:49:42.593 --> 00:49:44.260
AUDIENCE: So many
students here and many

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:45.968
of whom will be
graduating will get a job

00:49:45.968 --> 00:49:48.635
and, hopefully, we save
money for retirement,

00:49:48.635 --> 00:49:51.694
since that's what my
parents helped me realize.

00:49:51.694 --> 00:49:55.570
So with the lack of
regulation that is here now

00:49:55.570 --> 00:49:58.000
and, if, let's say, the
administration and the economy

00:49:58.000 --> 00:50:00.200
doesn't pack regulations
but it kills it,

00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:02.250
there might be more of
a boom and bust cycle.

00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:05.290
So what do you recommend
for next year's student?

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:08.320
KRUGMAN: Oh, gosh.

00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:10.180
I really hate doing
investment advice.

00:50:12.910 --> 00:50:17.250
I have to say, even with all
of that, it's a little early.

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:27.640
When I got my first job, I got
this gold-embossed envelope

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:28.390
on creamy paper.

00:50:28.390 --> 00:50:30.490
I thought, oh, here's
the actual contract.

00:50:30.490 --> 00:50:33.550
And it turned out to
be my retirement plan.

00:50:33.550 --> 00:50:35.040
And I was 24 years old.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:38.075
And really, it's too soon to
be thinking about retirement.

00:50:38.075 --> 00:50:38.950
Think about it later.

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:44.530
Not too much later,
but not at that age.

00:50:47.661 --> 00:50:49.953
AUDIENCE: So these firms that
still got a lot of money,

00:50:49.953 --> 00:50:52.770
but they don't invest
it, shouldn't we

00:50:52.770 --> 00:50:53.773
split up these firms?

00:50:53.773 --> 00:50:55.315
Also, in the long
run, the government

00:50:55.315 --> 00:50:58.303
can be hijacked again
by "too big to fail."

00:50:58.303 --> 00:50:58.970
KRUGMAN: Oh, OK.

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:00.280
AUDIENCE: How likely is it
that that's going to happen?

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:01.905
KRUGMAN: OK, the
question is, should we

00:51:01.905 --> 00:51:05.023
be breaking up "too big
to fail" financial firms?

00:51:05.023 --> 00:51:05.940
Is it going to happen?

00:51:11.342 --> 00:51:13.050
I don't have any
problem with breaking up

00:51:13.050 --> 00:51:15.040
big financial institutions.

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:17.730
I think it's not all clear
that there is any big benefit

00:51:17.730 --> 00:51:19.710
from having them.

00:51:19.710 --> 00:51:22.430
I don't believe that
they're at the essence

00:51:22.430 --> 00:51:23.430
of the financial crisis.

00:51:26.430 --> 00:51:31.170
And there are several
layers on that.

00:51:31.170 --> 00:51:40.118
One is that, even analytically,
our basic understanding of how

00:51:40.118 --> 00:51:41.910
it is when you have a
financial crisis, how

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:43.800
you can have bank
runs, does not hinge

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:45.280
on there being big players.

00:51:45.280 --> 00:51:47.850
You can have bank
runs even if there

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:50.100
are many, many small banks,
which is more or less what

00:51:50.100 --> 00:51:52.230
happened in the 1930s.

00:51:52.230 --> 00:51:56.917
So there's nothing to
prevent that from happening.

00:51:56.917 --> 00:51:58.500
Breaking up "too big
to fail" does not

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:02.250
insulate you from the chance
of a financial crisis.

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:05.167
Conversely, having banks
that are too big to fail

00:52:05.167 --> 00:52:07.000
is no guarantee that
you will be in trouble.

00:52:07.000 --> 00:52:10.230
So we can make that
argument various ways.

00:52:10.230 --> 00:52:13.670
But the easiest one, maybe,
is to just say, look,

00:52:13.670 --> 00:52:17.700
the stellar example right
now is Canada, which

00:52:17.700 --> 00:52:20.010
is really kind of amazing.

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:24.362
We should be looking to the
great moral and intellectual

00:52:24.362 --> 00:52:25.320
leadership from Canada.

00:52:28.990 --> 00:52:31.110
I discovered this Monday.

00:52:31.110 --> 00:52:33.360
I guess because there is
more and more Canadian stuff,

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:36.090
for some reason, appearing,
the Times has introduced

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:38.400
a new style rule, which
is that we are not,

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:40.163
in fact, allowed to
make fun of the way

00:52:40.163 --> 00:52:41.580
Canadians end their
sentences, eh.

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:48.330
But anyways, Canada
basically has five banks.

00:52:48.330 --> 00:52:49.830
Those five banks
dominate the scene,

00:52:49.830 --> 00:52:51.600
which means that, from the point
of view of the Canadian system,

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:53.580
all of their banks
are too big to fail.

00:52:53.580 --> 00:52:55.980
And that was not at the
core of the problem.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:03.510
If I were going to make a
strong argument for breaking up

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:06.420
big banks, it would be
that the big banks have

00:53:06.420 --> 00:53:09.090
too much political
influence, that they're

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:12.060
too big to constrain
politically, that they end up

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:16.020
being able to write legislation
on their own behalf, which

00:53:16.020 --> 00:53:19.650
is pretty much what
happened, more or less.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:24.880
Sandy Weill decreed the
end of Glass-Steagall.

00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:29.010
And the legislative process
was just a formality.

00:53:29.010 --> 00:53:32.285
So that's an issue,
although even small banks,

00:53:32.285 --> 00:53:33.660
through their
trade associations,

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:34.900
can do a lot of damage.

00:53:34.900 --> 00:53:44.340
But if I had to make a choice
between getting Paul Volcker's

00:53:44.340 --> 00:53:48.030
limitations on big banks
or Elizabeth Warren's

00:53:48.030 --> 00:53:51.120
choice of a proposal for a
Consumer Financial Protection

00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:56.520
Agency, I would take
Warren's agency in a second.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:59.370
I think it's much more important
to act on protecting consumers

00:53:59.370 --> 00:54:02.160
from predatory or
confusing lending.

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:06.870
It's more important to
have capital requirements.

00:54:06.870 --> 00:54:11.270
I actually think that the
bill that the House passed,

00:54:11.270 --> 00:54:14.370
the Barney Frank bill basically,
which addresses these things,

00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:16.470
but doesn't really
address too big to fail,

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:18.750
is a really good
piece of legislation.

00:54:18.750 --> 00:54:21.960
And I wish there was some
chance it would actually

00:54:21.960 --> 00:54:23.650
get through the Senate.

00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:33.240
So it's not an irrelevant
issue, but it's not

00:54:33.240 --> 00:54:34.302
the core of the problem.

00:54:34.302 --> 00:54:36.260
AUDIENCE: What about the
companies like GM or--

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:40.140
KRUGMAN: Oh, big companies.

00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:44.510
GM or C-- again, the size,
that has not really turned out

00:54:44.510 --> 00:54:45.630
to be a problem here.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:48.980
I mean, antitrust, yes,
and you want market power.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:54.140
One of the good things that's
happening behind the scenes

00:54:54.140 --> 00:54:56.750
is that antitrust
enforcement seems

00:54:56.750 --> 00:54:58.080
to actually be coming back now.

00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:01.850
But that's not at the core
of the macro problems.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:05.050
OK, go.

00:55:05.050 --> 00:55:05.617
Yeah.

00:55:05.617 --> 00:55:07.325
AUDIENCE: So if one
of your main question

00:55:07.325 --> 00:55:10.866
be how could this happen again,
isn't it just as possible

00:55:10.866 --> 00:55:14.100
that maybe because
the economy is cyclic,

00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:15.790
maybe it was a
mischaracterization

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:18.681
to say that we foolproofed
the economy such

00:55:18.681 --> 00:55:20.848
that it never happens again?

00:55:20.848 --> 00:55:22.640
KRUGMAN: So the question
is, wasn't it just

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:24.475
a mistake to think
that we could set up

00:55:24.475 --> 00:55:26.600
a system so that these
things wouldn't happen again

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:29.510
to the economy?

00:55:29.510 --> 00:55:32.930
The way I look at, actually,
is that we went 50 years

00:55:32.930 --> 00:55:34.310
without a big crisis.

00:55:34.310 --> 00:55:38.330
And we really only started
on the route to this crisis

00:55:38.330 --> 00:55:43.190
when we politically
and intellectually

00:55:43.190 --> 00:55:44.930
started to forget
about the old lessons.

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:50.480
As long as banks were
pretty heavily regulated,

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:55.250
as long as there was a
sort of proper suspicion

00:55:55.250 --> 00:55:59.990
of too much
creativity in finance,

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:02.640
we were relatively
safe from these things.

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:09.470
Now, it may be that there is
a kind of a larger logic that

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:12.740
says that these things
will happen eventually.

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:18.900
So people are
rediscovering Hyman Minsky,

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:21.290
a heterodox economist.

00:56:21.290 --> 00:56:24.410
The Minsky argument
is that there

00:56:24.410 --> 00:56:28.970
is a cycle in these things,
that after crises, people

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:31.520
become very cautious,
both in the private sector

00:56:31.520 --> 00:56:32.960
and public policy.

00:56:32.960 --> 00:56:35.533
And then that produces an
extended period of stability.

00:56:35.533 --> 00:56:37.700
And then people forget about
the risks in something.

00:56:37.700 --> 00:56:42.637
And then they revert to
all the bad old behaviors.

00:56:42.637 --> 00:56:44.720
That's basically all there
is in Minsky, actually.

00:56:44.720 --> 00:56:46.745
You can read all 400
pages, but that's

00:56:46.745 --> 00:56:47.870
what you'll get in the end.

00:56:51.513 --> 00:56:52.930
And there's probably
some of that.

00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:57.530
But I guess I always
come down to the thought

00:56:57.530 --> 00:57:04.610
that we did have an
extended period of stability

00:57:04.610 --> 00:57:06.290
without these things happening.

00:57:09.890 --> 00:57:12.195
This is not a deep
mystic secret about how

00:57:12.195 --> 00:57:13.320
you stop it from happening.

00:57:13.320 --> 00:57:16.030
We have individual
countries that have

00:57:16.030 --> 00:57:19.413
managed to largely avoid this.

00:57:19.413 --> 00:57:21.830
We even had some countries
have had terrible macro events,

00:57:21.830 --> 00:57:24.350
but still have relatively
stable banking systems,

00:57:24.350 --> 00:57:26.660
speaking of Spain.

00:57:26.660 --> 00:57:28.227
Now, given just
how bad things are,

00:57:28.227 --> 00:57:30.560
their banks might finally
just crumble under the strain.

00:57:30.560 --> 00:57:33.020
But at least so far, they've
done remarkably well.

00:57:35.855 --> 00:57:37.730
Sovereign must have
branches here too, right?

00:57:40.730 --> 00:57:42.883
Actually, if you call up
their telephone banking,

00:57:42.883 --> 00:57:44.300
you get the recorded
message which

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:46.820
says, Sovereign, now
part of Santander,

00:57:46.820 --> 00:57:48.590
one of the world's
strongest banks.

00:57:48.590 --> 00:57:49.898
So they're saying, trust us.

00:57:49.898 --> 00:57:50.690
We're not American.

00:57:50.690 --> 00:57:51.648
We're actually Spanish.

00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:56.280
And so you can do this.

00:57:56.280 --> 00:57:57.570
It's not that hard.

00:57:57.570 --> 00:58:04.820
But it does require that
you have the political will.

00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:07.070
The people who put together
the original banking

00:58:07.070 --> 00:58:12.260
regulations were smart,
but not brilliant geniuses.

00:58:12.260 --> 00:58:14.240
They were just
willing to say, we're

00:58:14.240 --> 00:58:18.440
going to sacrifice a little bit
on possibly optimum efficiency

00:58:18.440 --> 00:58:19.940
in order to limit the risks.

00:58:19.940 --> 00:58:23.022
And we just need to
recapture that sentiment.

00:58:25.602 --> 00:58:29.570
AUDIENCE: What if [INAUDIBLE]
management [INAUDIBLE]??

00:58:29.570 --> 00:58:30.362
KRUGMAN: All right.

00:58:30.362 --> 00:58:32.237
The question is whether
financial engineering

00:58:32.237 --> 00:58:36.090
can contribute to
growth and development?

00:58:36.090 --> 00:58:42.180
By the way, at Princeton, we
do not have a business school.

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:44.735
So where is our
finance department?

00:58:44.735 --> 00:58:46.110
And the answer
is, it actually is

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:47.280
in the engineering department.

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:49.440
We have something called
ORFE, Operations Research

00:58:49.440 --> 00:58:50.550
and Financial Engineering.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.920
And that's where our
finance people are.

00:58:52.920 --> 00:58:54.960
So I guess I'm
obliged to believe

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:57.780
in financial engineering.

00:58:57.780 --> 00:59:05.342
So there are levels on levels
of financial engineering.

00:59:08.850 --> 00:59:13.485
Bonds were a piece of financial
engineering when first created.

00:59:20.130 --> 00:59:22.830
Having some range of financial
instruments is a good thing.

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:28.680
Let me put it a
little differently.

00:59:28.680 --> 00:59:32.490
When we say we need to
safeguard financial innovation,

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:34.870
the question is, what
are we talking about?

00:59:34.870 --> 00:59:36.630
And it's almost a
parlor game now.

00:59:36.630 --> 00:59:40.590
Try to name a financial
innovation over the past 30

00:59:40.590 --> 00:59:45.150
years that has been
unambiguously a good thing.

00:59:45.150 --> 00:59:47.030
And you're not
allowed to use ATMs.

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:53.100
And it's virtually impossible
to come up with that.

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:54.630
You see many, many
speeches about,

00:59:54.630 --> 00:59:57.720
we mustn't cripple
financial innovation,

00:59:57.720 --> 01:00:00.180
but almost no concrete
examples-- or none,

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:01.080
as far as I know--

01:00:01.080 --> 01:00:03.635
of what it is that's
supposed to be a good thing.

01:00:03.635 --> 01:00:05.010
Five years ago,
people would have

01:00:05.010 --> 01:00:07.710
told you all of these
great subprime mortgages,

01:00:07.710 --> 01:00:13.380
the collateralized debt
obligations, credit default

01:00:13.380 --> 01:00:16.350
swaps, all of which have
blown up in our faces.

01:00:19.202 --> 01:00:20.910
Well I've written this
a number of times.

01:00:24.145 --> 01:00:25.770
Most of the evidence
points to the idea

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:29.860
that keeping banking boring
is really what we want to do.

01:00:29.860 --> 01:00:31.490
I'll probably take two more.

01:00:31.490 --> 01:00:33.255
One way up there.

01:00:33.255 --> 01:00:35.360
AUDIENCE: It's been said
of science that science

01:00:35.360 --> 01:00:37.270
progresses funeral by funeral.

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:40.650
Did this effect contribute to
the Dark Ages in economics?

01:00:40.650 --> 01:00:44.050
And could it possibly
contribute to a new Renaissance

01:00:44.050 --> 01:00:44.840
in economics?

01:00:44.840 --> 01:00:46.700
KRUGMAN: OK, yeah.

01:00:46.700 --> 01:00:47.700
People didn't hear that.

01:00:47.700 --> 01:00:49.570
If science progresses
funeral by funeral,

01:00:49.570 --> 01:00:51.360
what does this say about the
future of economics, roughly

01:00:51.360 --> 01:00:51.860
speaking?

01:01:00.208 --> 01:01:01.000
Let's put this way.

01:01:01.000 --> 01:01:03.375
I don't think that the funerals
are helping us right now.

01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:17.780
I don't want to go
too far with this.

01:01:17.780 --> 01:01:20.440
Let me say, as a preface,
that the great bulk of what

01:01:20.440 --> 01:01:22.480
economists do is
not on these issues.

01:01:26.920 --> 01:01:32.230
And the great bulk of it
is careful, empirical work

01:01:32.230 --> 01:01:36.490
on many things, or careful
analysis of problems

01:01:36.490 --> 01:01:39.410
that are not in the headlines.

01:01:39.410 --> 01:01:44.860
On macroeconomics,
what happened was

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:53.090
pretty clearly that, in the
wake of the big changes that

01:01:53.090 --> 01:01:57.840
took place in the
field in the 1970s,

01:01:57.840 --> 01:02:00.110
a lot of schools
basically stopped

01:02:00.110 --> 01:02:05.990
teaching old fashioned
macro, even when

01:02:05.990 --> 01:02:08.817
there was still a lot of
evidence that it was useful,

01:02:08.817 --> 01:02:10.400
even though there
were a lot of things

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:13.580
that people needed to know,
MIT being one of the places

01:02:13.580 --> 01:02:15.800
where you could still get it.

01:02:15.800 --> 01:02:20.450
But what has been really
startling within profession

01:02:20.450 --> 01:02:22.670
discussion was how
many people who

01:02:22.670 --> 01:02:26.300
are in their 30's
or 40's in economics

01:02:26.300 --> 01:02:33.570
simply never encountered
the idea of fiscal stimulus

01:02:33.570 --> 01:02:36.595
and the logic of why it might
work or why it might not.

01:02:36.595 --> 01:02:37.970
They just haven't
encountered it.

01:02:37.970 --> 01:02:41.930
So in this case, the
progression actually

01:02:41.930 --> 01:02:44.390
meant that people
who knew things

01:02:44.390 --> 01:02:47.600
that we needed to remember
disappeared from the profession

01:02:47.600 --> 01:02:52.760
and were not replaced.

01:02:52.760 --> 01:02:57.110
Hopefully, if we do end
up learning something

01:02:57.110 --> 01:03:01.100
from this crisis and the
ones that will surely follow,

01:03:01.100 --> 01:03:04.680
then there will be
future generations who

01:03:04.680 --> 01:03:06.680
bring that knowledge back
and bring other things

01:03:06.680 --> 01:03:08.640
that we don't yet know.

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:14.120
But I wish it were the case
that we had a clear progression

01:03:14.120 --> 01:03:14.720
in this field.

01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:16.760
And that's the shocking thing.

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:24.380
In many ways, it seems as if the
state of understanding of macro

01:03:24.380 --> 01:03:28.340
was better when I
was in grad school

01:03:28.340 --> 01:03:32.040
than it is in much of
the profession now.

01:03:32.040 --> 01:03:34.060
Boy, that's a
depressing thought.

01:03:34.060 --> 01:03:39.146
Let me take one more question,
then we'll call it to an end.

01:03:39.146 --> 01:03:41.098
AUDIENCE: Do you think
that global warming

01:03:41.098 --> 01:03:45.518
will make a financial
impact on [INAUDIBLE]??

01:03:45.518 --> 01:03:50.640
If there is no
Japan [INAUDIBLE]??

01:03:50.640 --> 01:03:52.535
KRUGMAN: I'm sorry.

01:03:52.535 --> 01:03:53.910
Do I think that
what will cause--

01:03:53.910 --> 01:03:55.800
AUDIENCE: The global warming.

01:03:55.800 --> 01:03:58.150
KRUGMAN: Global warming, oh.

01:03:58.150 --> 01:04:02.160
Will global warming
cause financial crisis?

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:06.495
Well, you know, climate
change is a slow process.

01:04:09.643 --> 01:04:11.310
That's not what has
me worried about it,

01:04:11.310 --> 01:04:13.410
though God knows it could.

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:23.910
But I think the reason to
worry about climate change

01:04:23.910 --> 01:04:26.200
is not that it will
cause a financial crisis.

01:04:26.200 --> 01:04:28.200
The reason to worry
about climate change

01:04:28.200 --> 01:04:32.160
is that is that low lying
countries will find themselves

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:34.470
underwater, that the
southwestern United States will

01:04:34.470 --> 01:04:36.140
turn into a permanent
dust bowl, and all

01:04:36.140 --> 01:04:40.980
of these other things, which
are not speculative, crazy,

01:04:40.980 --> 01:04:42.380
something Hollywood dreams up.

01:04:42.380 --> 01:04:48.640
Those are the central cases
in the major climate models.

01:04:48.640 --> 01:04:50.433
So that's the stuff
to worry about.

01:04:50.433 --> 01:04:51.850
There is a certain
sense in which,

01:04:51.850 --> 01:04:55.120
why does any of this matter if
we don't actually tackle that?

01:04:55.120 --> 01:04:59.480
But I guess you have
to deal with problems

01:04:59.480 --> 01:05:02.430
at different timescales
all at the same time.

01:05:02.430 --> 01:05:03.420
Thank you, everybody.

01:05:03.420 --> 01:05:07.070
[APPLAUSE]

